The three Phoenician towns", "text": "Thucydides says the Phoenicians had drawn together at Motya, Solus and Panormus, close to their Elymian allies.", "lon": -55.401, "lat": 0.217}, {"year": -579, "title": "Pentathlus killed", "text": "Elymians and Phoenicians defeat the Cnidians; Pentathlus falls, and the colony is abandoned (c. 580–576).", "lon": -60.221, "lat": -1.012}, {"year": -578, "title": "Lipara settled", "text": "Survivors led by Pentathlus's kin settle Lipara; Pausanias, citing Antiochus, places the failed colony at Pachynus instead.", "lon": -48.943, "lat": 2.34}, {"year": -575, "title": "Temple of Apollo", "text": "Syracuse raises one of the earliest stone Doric temples in the Greek west.", "lon": -47.397, "lat": -5.536}, {"year": -573, "title": "Tyre submits", "text": "Tyre yields to Babylon after a thirteen-year siege, and Carthage increasingly leads the western Phoenicians.", "lon": -69.998, "lat": -6.709}, {"year": -570, "title": "Phalaris of Akragas", "text": "Phalaris seizes power in Akragas; later writers remember his cruelty and his bronze bull.", "lon": -55.082, "lat": -4.196}, {"year": -560, "title": "Stesichorus of Himera", "text": "The poet Stesichorus, of Greek Himera on the north coast, sings of Heracles in the far west.", "lon": -54.082, "lat": -0.453}, {"year": -560, "title": "Panormus harbour", "text": "Phoenician Panormus, with its wide sheltered harbour, becomes the chief port of the northwest.", "lon": -56.174, "lat": 0.385}, {"year": -553, "title": "Camarina revolts", "text": "Camarina rises against its mother city Syracuse, with Sicel allies.", "lon": -51.217, "lat": -6.598}, {"year": -552, "title": "Camarina razed", "text": "Syracuse defeats the rebels, destroys Camarina and drives out its people.", "lon": -51.308, "lat": -6.486}, {"year": -550, "title": "Malchus in Sicily", "text": "The Carthaginian general Malchus wins much of Sicily, says Justin; the dates are very uncertain.", "lon": -56.31, "lat": 0.273}, {"year": -550, "title": "Temples of Selinus", "text": "Selinus begins its great Doric temples on the acropolis, among them Temple C.", "lon": -58.629, "lat": -2.576}, {"year": -550, "title": "Kerkouane", "text": "The Punic town of Kerkouane on Cape Bon grows, with its purple-dye works and fine houses.", "lon": -66.451, "lat": -6.151}, {"year": -550, "title": "Astarte at Tas-Silġ", "text": "On Malta, Phoenicians worship Astarte at the hilltop sanctuary of Tas-Silġ.", "lon": -50.717, "lat": -12.351}, {"year": -545, "title": "Malchus defeated", "text": "In Sardinia Malchus loses a great battle, and Carthage sends him into exile (c. 545–540).", "lon": -75.955, "lat": 5.244}, {"year": -544, "title": "Siege of Carthage", "text": "Malchus besieges Carthage, crucifies his own son and seizes the city, then is tried and executed.", "lon": -69.907, "lat": -6.653}, {"year": -540, "title": "Motya's walls", "text": "Motya rings its lagoon island with a stone wall and towers.", "lon": -60.221, "lat": -1.068}, {"year": -540, "title": "Mago's dynasty", "text": "Mago takes command at Carthage; his sons and grandsons lead its armies for three generations.", "lon": -70.089, "lat": -6.765}, {"year": -530, "title": "Wars in Sardinia", "text": "Mago's sons Hasdrubal and Hamilcar wage long wars to master Sardinia (c. 530–510).", "lon": -77.365, "lat": 6.25}, {"year": -530, "title": "Pythagoras at Croton", "text": "Pythagoras of Samos settles in Croton and founds his brotherhood.", "lon": -39.03, "lat": 5.747}, {"year": -530, "title": "Sanctuary of Eryx", "text": "On Mount Eryx, Elymians, Phoenicians and Greeks honour a goddess of love, Astarte to some, Aphrodite to others.", "lon": -59.675, "lat": -0.062}, {"year": -525, "title": "Carthage spared", "text": "In Egypt, Phoenician crews refuse the Persian king Cambyses' order to sail against Carthage, their own kin.", "lon": -70.043, "lat": -6.598}, {"year": -520, "title": "Cleomenes is king", "text": "Anaxandridas dies; the Spartans choose his eldest son Cleomenes over his half-brother Dorieus.", "lon": -14.929, "lat": -5.48}, {"year": -515, "title": "Dorieus sails", "text": "Without consulting Delphi, Dorieus sails from Laconia with Theraean guides, bound for Libya.", "lon": -14.337, "lat": -7.212}, {"year": -514, "title": "Colony on the Cinyps", "text": "Dorieus founds a colony by the Cinyps river, which Herodotus calls the fairest land in Libya.", "lon": -52.445, "lat": -30.84}, {"year": -512, "title": "Driven from Libya", "text": "In the third year, the Libyan Macae and the Carthaginians drive the Spartans out.", "lon": -52.263, "lat": -30.56}, {"year": -511, "title": "The oracle", "text": "Advised by Antichares of Eleon, Dorieus asks Delphi if he will win the land he sails for; the god says he will.", "lon": -14.61, "lat": 2.395}, {"year": -510, "title": "Sybaris destroyed", "text": "Croton crushes Sybaris; the Sybarites later claimed Dorieus helped, which the Crotoniates denied.", "lon": -39.166, "lat": 5.579}, {"year": -510, "title": "Philip of Croton", "text": "The exiled Olympic victor Philip of Croton joins Dorieus with a trireme crewed at his own expense.", "lon": -38.893, "lat": 5.859}, {"year": -510, "title": "Heraclea near Eryx", "text": "Dorieus lands to claim Eryx, the land Heracles was said to have won by wrestling its king.", "lon": -59.812, "lat": -0.174}, {"year": -510, "title": "Dorieus killed", "text": "Phoenicians and Segestans defeat the Spartans; Dorieus and three of his co-founders are killed.", "lon": -58.584, "lat": -0.621}, {"year": -510, "title": "Euryleon takes Minoa", "text": "Euryleon, the one surviving co-founder, seizes Minoa, a colony of Selinus.", "lon": -56.492, "lat": -3.693}, {"year": -510, "title": "Tyrant of Selinus", "text": "Euryleon helps topple Peithagoras of Selinus, rules as tyrant, and is killed at the altar of Zeus Agoraios.", "lon": -58.538, "lat": -2.688}, {"year": -510, "title": "A shrine for Philip", "text": "The Segestans honour the slain Philip, the most handsome Greek of his day, with a hero's shrine.", "lon": -58.493, "lat": -0.677}], "eras": [[-580, -571, "Pentathlus and the Cnidians"], [-570, -551, "Tyrants and Temples"], [-550, -521, "Carthage Takes the Lead"], [-520, -511, "The Exile of Dorieus"], [-510, -510, "Heraclea at Eryx"]], "refLine": null, "metrics": [{"label": "Phoenician & Elymian west", "stages": [1, 2]}, {"label": "Greek colonies", "stages": [3]}], "chartNote": "Share of the area of today's provinces of Sicily, Calabria and southern Sardinia, by the people who held each province's main towns. Regions are modern boundaries and most held several peoples at once; Greek Selinus, for example, lay in the same province as Elymian Segesta and Phoenician Motya. Dates are approximate and rest largely on Herodotus, Thucydides, Diodorus and Justin.", "chartTitle": "Who held the land, by area", "shares": [[0.0544, 0.0, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0529], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0], [0.0544, 0.0529, 0.2358, 0.657, 0.0